    {
      "id": "u2fDU8smV4Y54qCcLB_8M",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 907.2974600503946,
      "y": 1117.9611442025407,
      "width": 786.3995361328125,
      "height": 575,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0A",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 19749586,
      "version": 858,
      "versionNonce": 78736960,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1786685964459,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "** How user can automatically sync files across devices\n-- When a user updates a file on their local machine, \nwe need to sync these changes with the remote server. \nWe consider the remote server to be the source of truth, \nso it's important that we get it consistent as soon as possible\nso that other local devices can know when there are changes they should pull in.\n\n-- To do this:\n1) monitor local Dropbox folder for changes in OS-specific file system events\n2) detects changes, queue the modified file for upload locally\n3) use our upload API to send the changes to server along with updated\nmetadata\n4) Conflicts ares resolved using \"last write wins\" strategy.\n-- 2 options:\n1) Polling:\n-- periodic polling GET /file/changes?since={timestamp} to \ncatch any change we might miss (if web socket drop)\n2) Web socket or SSE: -- change events through websocket\nThe server maintains an open connection with each client \nand pushes notifications when changes occur. \nThis is more complex but provides real-time updates.\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "** How user can automatically sync files across devices\n-- When a user updates a file on their local machine, \nwe need to sync these changes with the remote server. \nWe consider the remote server to be the source of truth, \nso it's important that we get it consistent as soon as possible\nso that other local devices can know when there are changes they should pull in.\n\n-- To do this:\n1) monitor local Dropbox folder for changes in OS-specific file system events\n2) detects changes, queue the modified file for upload locally\n3) use our upload API to send the changes to server along with updated\nmetadata\n4) Conflicts ares resolved using \"last write wins\" strategy.\n-- 2 options:\n1) Polling:\n-- periodic polling GET /file/changes?since={timestamp} to \ncatch any change we might miss (if web socket drop)\n2) Web socket or SSE: -- change events through websocket\nThe server maintains an open connection with each client \nand pushes notifications when changes occur. \nThis is more complex but provides real-time updates.\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "XMhy3X11r8PWIryaJg2s8",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1715.019446941863,
